INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti public attribution, leaks, and DOJ case page", "source_url": "https://rewardsforjustice.net/rewards/conti/; https://www.cisecurity.org/insights/blog/the-conti-leaks-a-case-of-cybercrimes-commercialization; https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/2022/03/01/conti-ransomware-group-internal-chats-leaked-over-russia-ukraine-conflict/; https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdtn/conti-ransomware-2", "text": "Benchmark sources establish: Rewards for Justice describes Conti, also known as Wizard Spider, as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti, and reports more than 1,000 operations and over 400 victim organizations worldwide, including over 290 in the U.S.; these are government-reported assessments, not adjudicated findings. Conti typically stole and encrypted files, demanded ransom via an online portal, and threatened to sell or publish stolen data; demands could reach $25m. CIS describes Conti as a notorious RaaS using double extortion, with ties to TrickBot, and says ContiLeaks published internal chats after Conti publicly supported Russia following the February 2022 invasion. CIS reports leaked-chat evidence of business units and roles: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers, outsourced spamming, separate budgets, and an insider estimate of more than 60 employees in July 2021; it also reports spending on Cobalt Strike, security/AV software, and Windows 11 reverse engineering. CIS characterizes RaaS as leasing malware to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, often described as 70/30, and says Conti later dismantled/rebranded into smaller units and shut down leak/negotiation infrastructure in 2022. Rapid7 reports the Feb. 27, 2022 ContiLeaks release contained 14 MB of chat logs in 393 JSON files, some encrypted and unreadable, plus tools, exploits, and design discussions; it says the leak exposed recruitment, salary negotiations, product development, infrastructure, and alleged funding references, but information was incomplete and some claims were unverified. Rapid7 assessed Conti remained active after infrastructure takedowns and reported resumption of normal operations by Mar. 9, 2022. DOJ page identifies cases 3:23-CR-00092 (Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov) and 3:23-CR-00088 (Oleksii Lytvynenko). DOJ says the first four were charged in Sept. 2023 with conspiracy to violate CFAA and conspiracy to commit wire fraud; Lytvynenko was charged in Nov. 2025 with conspiracy to deploy Conti and later pleaded guilty to Count 2 (conspiracy to commit wire fraud) on June 10, 2026. DOJ explicitly states defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty, while recording the case linkage to the Conti ransomware conspiracy."}
